description:
lib.rs:38-43

  pub trait JmapMailExt {
      fn with_mail_session(&self, session: jmap_base_client::Session) -> methods::SessionClient;
  }

The trait is not sealed. A downstream crate can impl JmapMailExt for its own type:

  impl JmapMailExt for MyCustomClient { ... }

The trait exists solely to attach with_mail_session to jmap_base_client::JmapClient. There is no scenario where a third-party type should implement it — every JMAP Mail method on SessionClient depends on the concrete jmap_base_client::JmapClient inside SessionClient, so a custom impl can only fake the trait, never actually deliver the methods.

Consequence of unsealed: if a future revision of this crate adds another method to JmapMailExt (e.g. with_mail_session_v2 to take an updated Session shape), every downstream impl breaks. The crate's API contract therefore promises that JmapMailExt's method set is frozen at 0.1.0.

Standard idiom for "extension trait that should only be implemented by this crate" is the sealed-trait pattern:

  mod private { pub trait Sealed {} }
  pub trait JmapMailExt: private::Sealed {
      fn with_mail_session(...) -> SessionClient;
  }
  impl private::Sealed for jmap_base_client::JmapClient {}
  impl JmapMailExt for jmap_base_client::JmapClient { ... }

After sealing, adding methods to JmapMailExt becomes a NON-breaking change (no third party can impl the trait, so no third party breaks).

Same finding applies to the six sibling extension-client crates' Jmap*Ext traits (canonical-template propagation).

Recommendation: seal JmapMailExt before publish. Backward-compatible today (no third-party impls can exist while the crate is path-dep only). Post-publish, unsealing or breaking the seal becomes a major-version change.

Priority P3 because today no callers exist outside the workspace. But this is precisely the kind of immutability commitment that becomes expensive to fix after publish. The fix is cheap NOW and expensive LATER.

Mark says NO HUMAN — Recommendation: add the sealed-trait pattern. Standard Rust API idiom, no design ambiguity.
